1
00:00:00,830 --> 00:00:03,583
I haven't been on the dating scene

2
00:00:03,583 --> 00:00:06,294
since I was 22.

3
00:00:06,294 --> 00:00:10,048
But I still remember “the ick.”

4
00:00:10,048 --> 00:00:14,469
You know, that feeling when something just completely turned you off

5
00:00:14,469 --> 00:00:16,971
and there [was] no coming back from it?

6
00:00:16,971 --> 00:00:23,061
Turns out, bad dates are not the only thing with an ick problem.

7
00:00:23,061 --> 00:00:25,480
Politics has it too.

8
00:00:25,480 --> 00:00:26,689
I'm serious.

9
00:00:26,689 --> 00:00:28,441
The data proves it.

10
00:00:28,441 --> 00:00:32,028
When researchers at Pew a few years ago asked Americans

11
00:00:32,028 --> 00:00:34,530
to describe politics in one word,

12
00:00:34,530 --> 00:00:38,868
just two percent used a positive term.

13
00:00:38,868 --> 00:00:40,620
Two percent.

14
00:00:40,620 --> 00:00:41,954
Take a moment.

15
00:00:41,954 --> 00:00:43,998
Really think about it.

16
00:00:43,998 --> 00:00:49,378
When's the last time politics made you feel something other than exhausted,

17
00:00:49,378 --> 00:00:52,757
disgusted, afraid?

18
00:00:52,757 --> 00:00:53,966
I can't remember either.

19
00:00:53,966 --> 00:00:58,262
And I've worked in this business for 25 years.

20
00:00:58,262 --> 00:01:01,015
We're all feeling the ick.

21
00:01:01,015 --> 00:01:04,519
So we're doing digital detoxes,

22
00:01:04,519 --> 00:01:06,062
news cleanses,

23
00:01:06,062 --> 00:01:11,109
or just putting our phones in the drawer and calling it self-care.

24
00:01:11,109 --> 00:01:13,069
Good stuff.

25
00:01:13,069 --> 00:01:16,405
But tuning out altogether,

26
00:01:16,405 --> 00:01:20,701
that's when things start getting done to us, not for us.

27
00:01:20,701 --> 00:01:22,203
And we can't afford it.

28
00:01:22,203 --> 00:01:23,871
Not now.

29
00:01:23,871 --> 00:01:26,707
So what do we do?

30
00:01:26,707 --> 00:01:32,839
What if we made politics something people actually want to be part of?

31
00:01:32,839 --> 00:01:34,757
Let me tell you what I've learned.

32
00:01:34,757 --> 00:01:38,511
But first, what I had to unlearn.

33
00:01:38,511 --> 00:01:41,806
My journey starts where all problems go to get solved.

34
00:01:41,806 --> 00:01:43,432
Washington, DC.

35
00:01:43,432 --> 00:01:45,351
(Laughter)

36
00:01:45,351 --> 00:01:49,188
I like to think I was different than your typical political insider,

37
00:01:49,188 --> 00:01:50,982
and in many ways I was.

38
00:01:50,982 --> 00:01:55,361
I helped start an organization whose entire job was to watch Fox News

39
00:01:55,361 --> 00:01:56,571
so you don't have to.

40
00:01:56,571 --> 00:01:58,156
(Laughter)

41
00:01:58,156 --> 00:01:59,490
I'm proud of that work.

42
00:01:59,490 --> 00:02:00,992
Thank you, it mattered.

43
00:02:00,992 --> 00:02:04,579
(Applause)

44
00:02:04,579 --> 00:02:06,831
But it was all built out of think tanks

45
00:02:06,831 --> 00:02:09,709
where we thought it was our job to find the right answer

46
00:02:09,709 --> 00:02:12,211
and ship it out to everyone else.

47
00:02:12,211 --> 00:02:16,340
I even helped start a spinoff devoted entirely to message testing.

48
00:02:16,340 --> 00:02:20,845
I truly believed [that] if we could get everyone to repeat the perfect words,

49
00:02:20,845 --> 00:02:22,638
we'd win.

50
00:02:22,638 --> 00:02:26,851
And sometimes it sort of barely worked.

51
00:02:26,851 --> 00:02:29,770
Even after I moved to the burbs outside Cleveland

52
00:02:29,770 --> 00:02:32,231
to start a family around family,

53
00:02:32,231 --> 00:02:34,567
I kept doing the DC thing.

54
00:02:34,567 --> 00:02:40,156
I was living in Ohio, but my head was in Washington.

55
00:02:40,156 --> 00:02:43,993
Everything changed for me in 2018.

56
00:02:43,993 --> 00:02:47,872
The 2016 presidential election had lit a fire

57
00:02:47,872 --> 00:02:51,209
under suburban women all over the country.

58
00:02:51,209 --> 00:02:54,503
Women who had never done anything more than show up to vote

59
00:02:54,503 --> 00:02:56,797
had started organizing.

60
00:02:56,797 --> 00:02:58,591
And they got results.

61
00:02:58,591 --> 00:03:00,885
In a typical midterm election,

62
00:03:00,885 --> 00:03:05,264
the party in power loses about 26 congressional seats.

63
00:03:05,264 --> 00:03:08,392
In 2018, they lost 43,

64
00:03:08,392 --> 00:03:11,270
and 40 were suburban.

65
00:03:11,270 --> 00:03:14,899
Even more seats flipped locally in state legislatures.

66
00:03:14,899 --> 00:03:18,653
Some of it was happening in my own backyard.

67
00:03:18,653 --> 00:03:20,488
But I had no idea.

68
00:03:20,488 --> 00:03:24,533
I was too busy dialing in to DC conference calls,

69
00:03:24,533 --> 00:03:27,328
even though I had become part of the very demographic

70
00:03:27,328 --> 00:03:31,123
both political parties spend millions trying to reach:

71
00:03:31,123 --> 00:03:33,751
a suburban mom.

72
00:03:33,751 --> 00:03:38,881
The morning after that election, I sat on the floor of my bedroom closet.

73
00:03:38,881 --> 00:03:41,050
I'd just had my second child,

74
00:03:41,050 --> 00:03:45,304
and for the first time, I felt completely out of alignment.

75
00:03:45,304 --> 00:03:47,056
Lost.

76
00:03:47,056 --> 00:03:51,477
I knew I couldn't be a DC operative anymore.

77
00:03:51,477 --> 00:03:54,605
I wanted to find those women.

78
00:03:54,605 --> 00:03:58,609
In DC, they search for "real people."

79
00:03:58,609 --> 00:04:00,736
Seriously, that's what they refer to them as

80
00:04:00,736 --> 00:04:02,905
in meetings and memos.

81
00:04:02,905 --> 00:04:07,368
But they don't really know them or even really listen to them.

82
00:04:07,368 --> 00:04:08,744
I wanted to.

83
00:04:08,744 --> 00:04:10,663
So I called up people that I knew

84
00:04:10,663 --> 00:04:13,708
who were working locally in Ohio politics, and I asked,

85
00:04:13,708 --> 00:04:15,626
“Do you know any of these suburban women

86
00:04:15,626 --> 00:04:18,045
who are organizing in their own neighborhoods?”

87
00:04:18,045 --> 00:04:20,631
It turns out they did.

88
00:04:20,631 --> 00:04:22,800
So I hit the road.

89
00:04:22,800 --> 00:04:25,511
I met some over wine, some at a bookstore,

90
00:04:25,511 --> 00:04:26,679
one group over dinner,

91
00:04:26,679 --> 00:04:28,514
and every time, I thought,

92
00:04:28,514 --> 00:04:30,683
these women -- I want to hang out with them.

93
00:04:30,683 --> 00:04:33,477
They're fun, they're fired up.

94
00:04:33,477 --> 00:04:36,731
And they finish each other's sentences.

95
00:04:36,731 --> 00:04:39,817
Each time I’d ask, “How long have you all known each other?”

96
00:04:39,817 --> 00:04:43,237
I figured they’d say since high school or college.

97
00:04:43,237 --> 00:04:45,698
"Months," they'd say.

98
00:04:45,698 --> 00:04:48,409
"We've known each other for months."

99
00:04:48,409 --> 00:04:50,161
And that's when it hit me.

100
00:04:50,161 --> 00:04:53,789
They didn’t organize because they were friends --

101
00:04:53,789 --> 00:04:57,793
they were friends because they organized.

102
00:04:57,793 --> 00:04:58,961
Take Tara.

103
00:04:58,961 --> 00:05:00,796
She's a single mom.

104
00:05:00,796 --> 00:05:04,342
I was introduced to her by one of the women I'd met over wine, Stephanie.

105
00:05:04,342 --> 00:05:08,721
Tara was distraught over the division in our country.

106
00:05:08,721 --> 00:05:10,556
She wanted to do something about it.

107
00:05:10,556 --> 00:05:12,266
She just didn't know what yet.

108
00:05:12,266 --> 00:05:16,687
So I invited her over to my house along with a handful of other women.

109
00:05:16,687 --> 00:05:18,564
My mom made Palomas.

110
00:05:18,564 --> 00:05:23,069
I made her egg casserole, and we set up on my back deck.

111
00:05:23,069 --> 00:05:28,741
Before long, these women were opening up to each other in ways I didn't expect.

112
00:05:28,741 --> 00:05:32,286
One woman with a medically fragile child,

113
00:05:32,286 --> 00:05:35,331
who loved dinosaurs as much as my son,

114
00:05:35,331 --> 00:05:39,877
shared her sheer exhaustion from battling the health care system.

115
00:05:39,877 --> 00:05:42,463
Another, who had been raised hard-line Republican,

116
00:05:42,463 --> 00:05:45,966
was starting to question everything she'd been taught.

117
00:05:45,966 --> 00:05:49,553
There was laughter and there were tears.

118
00:05:49,553 --> 00:05:52,723
The kind that come when you let your guard down.

119
00:05:52,723 --> 00:05:58,104
And in the end, Tara said seven words I will never forget.

120
00:05:58,104 --> 00:06:02,066
"If this is politics, I'm all in."

121
00:06:02,066 --> 00:06:03,526
A few years later,

122
00:06:03,526 --> 00:06:07,488
Tara ran for school board and won.

123
00:06:07,488 --> 00:06:12,326
But this story is not about just one woman or one backyard.

124
00:06:12,326 --> 00:06:16,497
I started meeting women like Tara all over Ohio, all over the country.

125
00:06:16,497 --> 00:06:18,791
Women who'd never done anything political before

126
00:06:18,791 --> 00:06:21,752
were getting together and getting organized.

127
00:06:21,752 --> 00:06:24,713
And all of them had something DC can never get.

128
00:06:24,713 --> 00:06:28,384
Real networks, real trust.

129
00:06:28,384 --> 00:06:30,636
But none of them knew about each other.

130
00:06:30,636 --> 00:06:32,513
I wanted to connect them

131
00:06:32,513 --> 00:06:34,098
and help them tap into the power

132
00:06:34,098 --> 00:06:37,268
they were just beginning to realize they had.

133
00:06:37,268 --> 00:06:41,856
So I hired two of the women from my road trip, and we got to work.

134
00:06:41,856 --> 00:06:43,190
Our first rule:

135
00:06:43,190 --> 00:06:46,902
if anything we did or said sounded like a political party,

136
00:06:46,902 --> 00:06:49,613
we'd throw it out and start over.

137
00:06:49,613 --> 00:06:51,574
Starting with the name.

138
00:06:51,574 --> 00:06:55,453
We called it "Red, Wine and Blue."

139
00:06:55,453 --> 00:06:57,538
The wine is optional.

140
00:06:57,538 --> 00:07:02,710
Diet Coke, Spindrift, Palomas and egg casserole, whatever.

141
00:07:02,710 --> 00:07:06,172
But how things got done needed an overhaul too.

142
00:07:06,172 --> 00:07:08,382
Traditional campaigns have their volunteers

143
00:07:08,382 --> 00:07:10,176
knock on strangers' doors

144
00:07:10,176 --> 00:07:12,094
and call people they've never met.

145
00:07:12,094 --> 00:07:13,471
We do the opposite.

146
00:07:13,471 --> 00:07:16,474
You see, every single one of us here

147
00:07:16,474 --> 00:07:18,267
already have a powerful network:

148
00:07:18,267 --> 00:07:20,144
friends, neighbors, coworkers,

149
00:07:20,144 --> 00:07:23,564
people from your book club, the school pick-up line.

150
00:07:23,564 --> 00:07:24,773
So we start there.

151
00:07:24,773 --> 00:07:27,735
We map out everyone we know who could be with us,

152
00:07:27,735 --> 00:07:31,614
and we take responsibility for making sure they vote.

153
00:07:31,614 --> 00:07:34,992
Then those people talk to their people and on and on.

154
00:07:34,992 --> 00:07:36,744
Sounds simple,

155
00:07:36,744 --> 00:07:38,954
but you don't want to make things awkward.

156
00:07:38,954 --> 00:07:42,541
What do you say to the friend who says, “I don’t do politics?”

157
00:07:42,541 --> 00:07:46,128
Or the neighbor who says, “Voting doesn’t matter?”

158
00:07:46,128 --> 00:07:49,673
This is where the friendship part comes in.

159
00:07:49,673 --> 00:07:51,425
We have happy hours

160
00:07:51,425 --> 00:07:54,929
or unhappy hours, depending on the day.

161
00:07:54,929 --> 00:07:57,515
We do trivia nights, banned book bingo --

162
00:07:57,515 --> 00:07:58,974
we learn together

163
00:07:58,974 --> 00:08:01,644
and get to know each other as people.

164
00:08:01,644 --> 00:08:05,189
So when it comes time to have those hard conversations,

165
00:08:05,189 --> 00:08:06,982
we know we're not doing it alone.

166
00:08:06,982 --> 00:08:10,486
We've practiced, we've gotten brave together.

167
00:08:10,486 --> 00:08:14,907
And that's the thing no political campaign can manufacture.

168
00:08:14,907 --> 00:08:16,992
Not the message --

169
00:08:16,992 --> 00:08:18,994
the nerve.

170
00:08:18,994 --> 00:08:21,497
That alone would be worth it.

171
00:08:21,497 --> 00:08:23,999
But you know what makes it even better?

172
00:08:23,999 --> 00:08:25,417
It works.

173
00:08:25,417 --> 00:08:28,796
When we hired an independent analyst to run the numbers,

174
00:08:28,796 --> 00:08:30,089
they ran them.

175
00:08:30,089 --> 00:08:31,924
Then they ran them again.

176
00:08:31,924 --> 00:08:34,051
They thought there had to be a mistake.

177
00:08:34,051 --> 00:08:36,720
I thought there had to be a mistake.

178
00:08:36,720 --> 00:08:38,681
Because here's what they showed.

179
00:08:38,681 --> 00:08:41,308
When our members reach out to the people they know --

180
00:08:41,308 --> 00:08:43,936
not strangers, the people they know --

181
00:08:43,936 --> 00:08:46,230
voter turnout among the people they contact

182
00:08:46,230 --> 00:08:49,024
goes up five to seven percentage points.

183
00:08:49,024 --> 00:08:50,901
Now listen, campaigns spend millions

184
00:08:50,901 --> 00:08:54,613
to move that number by a fraction of a point.

185
00:08:54,613 --> 00:08:59,910
A conversation between friends moves it by five.

186
00:08:59,910 --> 00:09:01,620
Now here's the best part.

187
00:09:01,620 --> 00:09:05,416
Friendship doesn’t go away after Election Day.

188
00:09:05,416 --> 00:09:10,713
So those same women are working together right now in their communities

189
00:09:10,713 --> 00:09:12,339
to stand up to ICE,

190
00:09:12,339 --> 00:09:14,258
take on AI data centers

191
00:09:14,258 --> 00:09:15,426
and school boards

192
00:09:15,426 --> 00:09:18,262
trying to ban books and take down pride flags.

193
00:09:18,262 --> 00:09:22,558
And they come from every side of the aisle.

194
00:09:22,558 --> 00:09:27,438
So if you're sitting in this room right now thinking

195
00:09:27,438 --> 00:09:29,523
that sounds appealing --

196
00:09:29,523 --> 00:09:32,359
but also overwhelming --

197
00:09:32,359 --> 00:09:34,945
I am talking to you.

198
00:09:34,945 --> 00:09:37,531
I know it's a hard moment to feel hopeful.

199
00:09:37,531 --> 00:09:40,826
I feel it too.

200
00:09:40,826 --> 00:09:43,412
But I promise you, no matter where you live,

201
00:09:43,412 --> 00:09:46,332
there are people who feel like you do right around the corner.

202
00:09:46,332 --> 00:09:48,751
And if you've never done anything political before,

203
00:09:48,751 --> 00:09:51,837
you are exactly who this moment needs most.

204
00:09:51,837 --> 00:09:54,381
Because the answers are not in DC --

205
00:09:54,381 --> 00:09:59,803
they are in living rooms and backyards among people who are getting together

206
00:09:59,803 --> 00:10:04,933
and making their communities the kinds of places they want to live in.

207
00:10:04,933 --> 00:10:09,730
And when we do it together, as friends who have each other's backs

208
00:10:09,730 --> 00:10:13,233
and show up for each other even when it's hard,

209
00:10:13,233 --> 00:10:17,446
that's what makes politics feel good.

210
00:10:17,446 --> 00:10:20,824
And that's something people want to be part of

211
00:10:20,824 --> 00:10:24,745
and something that actually works for all of us.

212
00:10:24,745 --> 00:10:28,624
So find your people,

213
00:10:28,624 --> 00:10:30,584
get together,

214
00:10:30,584 --> 00:10:34,213
start talking about what matters to you.

215
00:10:34,213 --> 00:10:37,466
Because as it turns out,

216
00:10:37,466 --> 00:10:40,886
we don't have to choose between taking care of our world

217
00:10:40,886 --> 00:10:43,389
and taking care of ourselves

218
00:10:43,389 --> 00:10:46,975
if we just do it together.

219
00:10:46,975 --> 00:10:48,143
Thank you.

220
00:10:48,143 --> 00:10:52,143
(Applause)
